<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro II of Kongo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1575–<a href="/years/1624">1624</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Pedro II Nkanga a Mvika, Pedro II Afonso</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro II Nkanga a Mvika was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> from 1622 to 1624, the first of the Kinkanga lineage, known as the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsundi">Nsundi</a>, and a descendant of Alfonso I on his mother's side. Formerly Duke of Mbamba, he was elected as a compromise candidate on the death of Álvaro III. The governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, João Correia de Sousa, invaded Kongo with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbangala">Imbangala</a> allies, but Pedro's army crushed them at Mbanda Kasi and drove them out; his protests to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> led to Sousa's recall and the return of some 1,200 slaves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. He proposed an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> and died in 1624, succeeded by his son Garcia I.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pedro was born in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsundi">Nsundi</a>, from which his father also came, and his royal house takes its name from it. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a> he is Nkanga a Mbika lua Ntumba a Mwemba, and in Portuguese Dom Pedro II Afonso. He served in the provincial government of King Álvaro III Nimi a Mpanzu, first as Marquis of Wembo. Álvaro III was a young man with older uncles who wished to rule and had no heir apparent. Pedro was widely regarded as a virtuous man and a model Christian, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, newly arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, held him up as an example.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As soon as he came to the throne, Governor João Correia de Sousa sent an army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, claiming the right to choose the king and accusing Pedro of harbouring runaway slaves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> while Duke of Mbamba. Fresh from a victory over Nambu a Ngongo, a southern neighbour of Kongo, a Portuguese army of 20,000 with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbangala">Imbangala</a> allies entered Mbamba. At Mbumbi it defeated and killed the new Duke of Mbamba and the Marquis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpemba">Mpemba</a>, who were badly outnumbered; both were later eaten by the Imbangala troops.</p><p>Pedro declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> an enemy of the state and led the main army south. After the victory at Mbanda Kasi, anti-Portuguese riots broke out across the kingdom, and Portuguese residents were disarmed and even made to give up their clothes. Pedro, anxious not to alienate the merchant community, which had generally stayed loyal, did what he could to protect their lives and property, so that some of his detractors called him &quot;King of Portuguese&quot;. Protests by Portuguese merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> and Angola added weight to his letters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pedro also wrote to the Dutch States General proposing that the newly formed West India Company help him attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>, the BaKongo by land and the Dutch by sea. He died on 13 April 1624 before the alliance could be made. The understanding between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> lasted, however, and came to fruition in 1641 with the Dutch attack on Luanda.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro II of Kongo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">M'banza-Kongo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">M'banza-Kongo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-6.27,14.25&amp;year=1606" aria-label="M'banza-Kongo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4945 2398 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pedro II of Kongo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5395 2668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5395 2668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-6.27,14.25&amp;year=1606">What was here in 1606? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
